WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.408 --> 00:00:07.633
Albert Einstein is by far the best-known scientist of the twentieth century.

2
00:00:08.213 --> 00:00:10.355
His name and image are symbols of genius.

3
00:00:11.035 --> 00:00:17.859
Einstein's scientific contributions changed the way people understand the workings of the universe and will continue to impact

4
00:00:17.899 --> 00:00:19.240
the world for years to come.

5
00:00:19.881 --> 00:00:24.904
It all started with his curiosity and his belief that "imagination is more important than knowledge."

6
00:00:25.468 --> 00:00:32.711
Born in 1879 in Ulm, Germany, Albert was the son of middle-class Jewish parents, Hermann and Pauline Einstein.

7
00:00:33.212 --> 00:00:38.434
His abilities were not immediately obvious; he was even slow to start speaking as a young boy.

8
00:00:39.014 --> 00:00:44.837
During his early education, many of his teachers considered him a lazy dreamer because he did not like

9
00:00:44.877 --> 00:00:47.258
the rote learning methods used in his German school.

10
00:00:47.918 --> 00:00:51.460
But he had a talent for numbers and a strong interest in the sciences.

11
00:00:52.444 --> 00:00:59.088
Einstein's father and uncle started a business making electrical devices, such as motors, which fascinated Albert.

12
00:00:59.509 --> 00:01:03.951
When his father showed him a compass, Albert was amazed that the needle always pointed north.

13
00:01:04.452 --> 00:01:08.614
He wanted to know more about the unseen forces called "electricity" and "magnetism."

14
00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:15.298
His uncle Jacob was a powerful influence on young Einstein, teaching him to use his imagination to come

15
00:01:15.338 --> 00:01:17.760
up with explanations for the phenomena around him.

16
00:01:18.368 --> 00:01:25.050
To help the child solve math problems and understand scientific theories, Jacob would use examples from everyday life

17
00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:27.491
to make difficult concepts understandable.

18
00:01:28.051 --> 00:01:32.253
Einstein loved the idea that mathematical formulas could describe reality.

19
00:01:33.013 --> 00:01:36.034
When Einstein was 15, his family left Germany.

20
00:01:36.674 --> 00:01:41.216
He attended high school in Switzerland, at a school where he was encouraged to explore his interests.

21
00:01:41.928 --> 00:01:47.152
It was during this time that Einstein first visualized traveling alongside a beam of light and wrote his

22
00:01:47.192 --> 00:01:48.413
first scientific paper.

23
00:01:49.073 --> 00:01:55.397
After completing high school, Einstein enrolled at a respected university in Zurich, now known as the Swiss Federal

24
00:01:55.457 --> 00:01:56.858
Institute of Technology.

25
00:01:57.379 --> 00:02:03.103
He angered some of his professors by frequently cutting classes and using one of his classmates' lecture notes.

26
00:02:03.863 --> 00:02:09.647
And without positive letters of recommendation from his instructors, Einstein could not get a job when he graduated

27
00:02:09.687 --> 00:02:09.927
in 1900.

28
00:02:11.528 --> 00:02:16.430
Finally, in 1902, he got a job at the Swiss Patent Office in Bern through a friend.

29
00:02:16.991 --> 00:02:22.833
Einstein spent his days examining applications for patents on new inventions and deducing whether a device would work.

30
00:02:23.353 --> 00:02:26.975
The job was enjoyable and allowed him to refine his own scientific theories.

31
00:02:27.595 --> 00:02:31.857
But since he did not have access to a laboratory, he performed "thought experiments."

32
00:02:32.617 --> 00:02:38.100
In these "experiments," he imagined a situation and reasoned through the processes that could affect its outcome.

33
00:02:38.752 --> 00:02:42.195
He would jot down ideas on sheets of paper he kept in his desk drawer.

34
00:02:42.816 --> 00:02:46.979
He joked that the drawer was his department of theoretical physics.

35
00:02:47.119 --> 00:02:51.943
In 1905, Einstein returned to his thought experiment of riding a beam of light.

36
00:02:52.644 --> 00:02:59.610
He imagined riding in a streetcar at the speed of light—around 300,000 kilometers per second—away from the clock

37
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:00.391
tower in Bern.

38
00:03:00.951 --> 00:03:05.155
If he looked back at the clock, it would appear to have stopped, but the watch in his

39
00:03:05.215 --> 00:03:06.516
pocket would function normally.

40
00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:11.101
Einstein later described this revelation as "a storm" that "broke loose in my mind."

41
00:03:11.581 --> 00:03:15.843
He had realized that if something travels at a speed close to that of light, then the flow

42
00:03:15.883 --> 00:03:17.263
of time can appear to change.

43
00:03:17.963 --> 00:03:21.985
So, if a ship were traveling at such a speed, time on the ship would appear to slow

44
00:03:22.025 --> 00:03:23.505
down to someone standing still.

45
00:03:24.345 --> 00:03:30.487
Scientists proved this concept in 1971 by sending a jet around the world at six hundred miles per

46
00:03:30.527 --> 00:03:33.996
hour, with an extremely accurate clock onboard.

47
00:03:34.016 --> 00:03:38.500
When the jet landed, the clock was slightly behind an identical clock sitting still on earth.

48
00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:42.923
Einstein's realization about time marked the beginning of his rise.

49
00:03:43.563 --> 00:03:48.167
Scientific thinkers up until this time had thought that time, space, and matter were all fixed.

50
00:03:48.847 --> 00:03:54.572
Einstein's theory led him to ask: if time and space could be affected by speed, why not matter

51
00:03:54.612 --> 00:03:55.112
and energy?

52
00:03:55.933 --> 00:04:02.878
In response, he devised the famous equation E mc2 (where E is energy, m is mass, and c

53
00:04:02.918 --> 00:04:03.919
is the speed of light).

54
00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:10.545
According to the formula, since the speed of light is constant, mass and energy must be interchangeable.

55
00:04:11.205 --> 00:04:17.831
In the fall of 1905, he published an astounding scientific article introducing his "theory of special relativity."

56
00:04:18.531 --> 00:04:24.476
That same year, Einstein received his doctoral degree and published a number of other groundbreaking articles.

57
00:04:25.237 --> 00:04:26.698
He was 26 years old.

58
00:04:27.659 --> 00:04:33.783
Over the following years, Einstein's ideas slowly came to the attention of the scientific community, and he became

59
00:04:33.823 --> 00:04:34.984
increasingly respected.

60
00:04:35.844 --> 00:04:42.328
In 1914, Einstein became a professor of theoretical physics at the University of Berlin in Germany.

61
00:04:43.169 --> 00:04:46.891
One question filled his mind—what could explain the force of gravity?

62
00:04:47.732 --> 00:04:54.075
In 1915, he finalized his "theory of general relativity," stating that gravity can be explained as a bending

63
00:04:54.115 --> 00:04:54.616
of space.

64
00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.723
One way to imagine this effect would be to think of a bowling ball placed on a bed.

65
00:05:00.283 --> 00:05:04.686
The bowling ball is heavy, so it will sink a little bit, creating a dip in the bed.

66
00:05:05.327 --> 00:05:09.830
If a smaller ball is then placed on the same bed, it will roll down into the dip,

67
00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:11.071
toward the bowling ball.

68
00:05:11.851 --> 00:05:19.056
Einstein theorized that gravity pulls objects toward the earth because space bends—just as the bed's surface bends under

69
00:05:19.096 --> 00:05:19.737
the bowling ball.

70
00:05:21.058 --> 00:05:26.276
The theory predicted that gravity would affect light in the same way, forcing it to bend around objects

71
00:05:26.316 --> 00:05:28.117
with a large mass.

72
00:05:28.257 --> 00:05:34.639
In 1919, astronomers studying a solar eclipse announced that their measurements supported Einstein's theory.

73
00:05:35.179 --> 00:05:41.701
He became an instant celebrity and, in 1921, was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics.

74
00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:48.422
In 1932, Adolf Hitler and the Nazi party were coming into power in Germany, and Jewish intellectuals were

75
00:05:48.462 --> 00:05:49.503
in serious danger.

76
00:05:50.223 --> 00:05:54.024
Einstein fled to the United States, settling in Princeton, New Jersey.

77
00:05:54.760 --> 00:06:00.725
At the Institute for Advanced Study, he began looking for a mathematical explanation that would describe all the

78
00:06:00.765 --> 00:06:04.849
forces of nature, a concept he called "unified field theory."

79
00:06:05.849 --> 00:06:12.675
By this time, physicists had confirmed Einstein's prediction that mass could be converted to energy, and many were

80
00:06:12.715 --> 00:06:16.138
concerned that this technology would be used to create a weapon.

81
00:06:16.839 --> 00:06:21.142
At the start of World War II in Europe, Einstein signed a famous letter to U.S.

82
00:06:21.182 --> 00:06:22.283
President Franklin D.

83
00:06:22.303 --> 00:06:25.789
Roosevelt, warning that Germany could be building an atom bomb.

84
00:06:26.449 --> 00:06:33.152
Although Einstein was a pacifist, he was concerned about Germany's aggressive tactics and believed that the United States

85
00:06:33.252 --> 00:06:34.713
must develop the weapon first.

86
00:06:35.513 --> 00:06:43.176
After the Americans devastated two Japanese cities with nuclear explosives, Albert Einstein returned to his normally pacifist views,

87
00:06:43.916 --> 00:06:47.538
speaking out against weapons of mass destruction and war in general.

88
00:06:48.358 --> 00:06:54.133
Einstein spent the next years of his life working with antiwar groups, giving speeches on the need for

89
00:06:54.193 --> 00:06:57.235
civil rights, and expanding his unified field theory.

90
00:06:58.076 --> 00:07:04.901
The creative genius died of heart failure on April 18, 1955, before he could fully develop the theory.

91
00:07:05.742 --> 00:07:12.326
Nevertheless, Einstein's ideas continue to serve as the basis for modern research and to be an inspiration for

92
00:07:12.386 --> 00:07:13.908
scientists all over the world.

93
00:07:14.588 --> 00:07:19.692
His curiosity and imagination truly changed the way people understand the universe.
